# Line endings and per-path behaviour for THIS REPOSITORY.
# A committed contract -- not core.autocrlf, which is per-machine and unenforceable.
# Verify with:  git ls-files --eol
# After editing this file:  git add --renormalize . && git status && commit ALONE.
#
# ONE LINE PER EXTENSION. `*.{cmd,bat}` matches NOTHING here -- brace expansion is
# not supported in .gitattributes, though it IS required in .editorconfig.
# Keep the two files in sync.

# ---- baseline: LF in the index AND in the working tree, on every platform.
# `eol=lf` is deliberate and is NOT redundant with text=auto. Without it the
# working-tree ending falls to each machine's core.eol, which defaults to
# `native` -- CRLF on Windows -- so a checkout here would disagree with the
# `end_of_line = lf` this repo's .editorconfig promises, and the editor and Git
# would quietly fight over every file. Declaring it here makes the contract
# committed and reviewable instead of per-machine, and gives a Windows checkout
# bytes identical to Linux, WSL and the container images. Windows-only paths
# opt back out in the eol=crlf block below.
*                   text=auto eol=lf

# ---- run by a POSIX interpreter: LF on EVERY platform, incl. Windows checkouts.
# These are COPY'd into containers; a CRLF shebang is `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`.
*.sh                text eol=lf
*.bash              text eol=lf
Dockerfile          text eol=lf
Dockerfile.*        text eol=lf
*.dockerfile        text eol=lf
entrypoint*         text eol=lf
justfile            text eol=lf
tools/scripts/check-license-headers text eol=lf

# ---- Windows-only: CRLF in the working tree on EVERY platform, incl. Linux CI.
# cmd.exe genuinely mis-executes LF-only batch files; PowerShell signatures cover
# exact bytes. No such file is tracked yet -- these are for the Windows port.
*.bat               text eol=crlf
*.cmd               text eol=crlf
*.ps1               text eol=crlf
*.psm1              text eol=crlf
*.psd1              text eol=crlf
*.sln               text eol=crlf

# ---- diff quality: hunk headers that name the enclosing item.
# Only Git's built-in driver names work; an unknown one is silently ignored.
*.rs                diff=rust
*.md                diff=markdown
*.sh                diff=bash

# ---- generated: keep the lockfiles out of review and out of language stats
*.lock              -diff linguist-generated=true
